22.4 Reporting a Bayesian analysis
A Bayesian result is only interpretable alongside the choices that produced it. Reporting guidelines ask for the following, which together let a reader judge and reproduce the analysis:

the model, written out, and the reason for each prior, with a prior predictive check where helpful;
the software, sampler settings (chains, iterations, warm-up) and convergence diagnostics (R-hat, effective sample sizes, any divergences);
posterior summaries: medians and credible intervals, and posterior probabilities for the questions of interest;
posterior predictive checks of fit;
a sensitivity analysis under alternative reasonable priors;
the data and code.




CHow it’s reported





Further reading: Kruschke (2021) set out the Bayesian analysis reporting guidelines; Gelman, Vehtari, et al. (2020) describe the full Bayesian workflow; Spiegelhalter et al. (2004) on reporting Bayesian analyses in health research.

1.5 Crude rates mislead: age standardization
Older people visit the emergency department more often. A district with many older residents will have a high crude rate even if each age group there is no worse off than elsewhere. Direct standardization asks: what would each district’s rate be if it had the same age structure as a common standard population? Here the standard is the whole city.


  Crude ratesAge-standardized rates

  Bars are sorted by the selected measure. The number on the left is the district's rank. The figure on the right is its share of residents aged 65+.


  Look inside a district







AStandard notation

\[r_{jk} = \frac{D_{jk}}{PT_{jk}} \qquad w_k = \frac{N_k^{\text{std}}}{\sum_{k'} N_{k'}^{\text{std}}}\] \[ASR_j = \sum_k w_k\, r_{jk}\]

\(j\) = district, \(k\) = age group. The crude rate weights each \(r_{jk}\) by district \(j\)’s own age structure instead of \(w_k\).




BModel list and R

std_w &lt;- visits |&gt;
  distinct(district, age_group, population) |&gt;
  group_by(age_group) |&gt;
  summarise(n = sum(population)) |&gt;
  mutate(w = n / sum(n))

visits |&gt;
  group_by(district, age_group) |&gt;
  summarise(r = 1000 * sum(visits) /
              (sum(population) / 365.25)) |&gt;
  left_join(std_w, by = "age_group") |&gt;
  group_by(district) |&gt;
  summarise(asr = sum(w * r))



CHow it’s reported



Always name the standard population. Standardized rates are only comparable when they use the same one.









NoteR code (optional): crude and age-standardized rates






std_w &lt;- visits |&gt;
  distinct(district, age_group, population) |&gt;
  group_by(age_group) |&gt;
  summarise(n = sum(population), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(w = n / sum(n))

rates &lt;- visits |&gt;
  group_by(district, age_group) |&gt;
  summarise(visits = sum(visits), py = sum(population) / 365.25, .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(rate = 1000 * visits / py)

rates |&gt;
  left_join(std_w, by = "age_group") |&gt;
  group_by(district) |&gt;
  summarise(crude = 1000 * sum(visits) / sum(py),
            asr   = sum(w * rate), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  arrange(desc(asr))

